    "stem": "Omar knows that $(8, 15, 17)$ and $(16, 30, 34)$ both satisfy $a^{2}+b^{2}=c^{2}$.\n\nWhich statements correctly distinguish a primitive Baudhāyana triple from a scaled triple?",
    "item_type": "three_statement",
    "options": {
      "A": "1 and 2 only",
      "B": "1 and 3 only",
      "C": "2 and 3 only",
      "D": "1, 2 and 3"
    },
    "statements": [
      {
        "id": "1",
        "text": "$(8, 15, 17)$ is primitive because $8$, $15$, and $17$ have no common factor greater than $1$."
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "text": "$(16, 30, 34)$ is not primitive because $2$ divides $16$, $30$, and $34$."
      },
      {
        "id": "3",
        "text": "$(16, 30, 34)$ must be primitive because scaling $(8, 15, 17)$ by $2$ still gives $a^{2}+b^{2}=c^{2}$."
      }
